    "body": "### Connector\n\nFivetran\n\n### Feature area\n\nLineage\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nWhen several services in `lineageInformation.dbServiceNames` hold a table with the same database, schema and name, the Fivetran connector attaches every connector's lineage to the first listed service.\n\n`_resolve_table` in `pipeline/fivetran/metadata.py` loops over `dbServiceNames` in order and returns the first service where `<service>.<database or *>.<schema>.<table>` exists. It never uses which source the Fivetran connector actually reads from. Sharded or per-tenant estates, where several databases share one schema, are the common case.\n\nIn our instance six MySQL services hold the same `evo` schema, one per Fivetran connector. With all six in one agent's `dbServiceNames`, all six connectors' 273 table edges were attached to the first service. Nothing was logged and the run reported success.\n\n### To Reproduce\n\n1. Two database services, A and B, each with table `db.schema.t`.\n2. Two Fivetran connectors, one syncing from A and one from B.\n3. A Fivetran agent with `dbServiceNames: [A, B]`.\n4. Both connectors' lineage originates from A.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe source service is chosen from the Fivetran connector's own source (for example its configured host, or its database/service type), or the ambiguity is reported and the edge skipped rather than bound to the first match.\n\nOur workaround is one Fivetran agent per source service, each with a `pipelineFilterPattern` and only its own service in `dbServiceNames`.\n\n### OS\n\nKubernetes (ingestion-base image)\n\n### Python version\n\n3.12\n\n### OpenMetadata version\n\n2.0.6\n\n### OpenMetadata Ingestion package version\n\nopenmetadata-ingestion==2.0.1.0; same logic on main.\n\n### Pre-submission checklist\n\n- [x] I searched for duplicate issues.\n- [x] I removed credentials, hostnames, emails, and other sensitive data from logs and config.\n",
